1
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:05,564
International student numbers are at a record high.

2
00:00:05,684 --> 00:00:07,834
You've probably seen that headline.

3
00:00:07,954 --> 00:00:09,885
But it hides a simple question.

4
00:00:10,005 --> 00:00:11,741
Who is actually arriving?

5
00:00:11,861 --> 00:00:15,902
This is The Price of Arrival, a data story by DataLens.

6
00:00:16,602 --> 00:00:17,691
Meet Mei.

7
00:00:17,811 --> 00:00:22,091
She's 23, and she's thinking about a Master's degree in Sydney.

8
00:00:22,211 --> 00:00:24,100
She has four big questions.

9
00:00:24,220 --> 00:00:26,359
Is "record high" even about me?

10
00:00:26,479 --> 00:00:28,266
Where would I study and live?

11
00:00:28,386 --> 00:00:30,218
When is it safe to apply?

12
00:00:30,338 --> 00:00:31,866
And can I afford it?

13
00:00:31,986 --> 00:00:34,080
Getting this wrong is expensive.

14
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:39,496
The visa fee jumped from A$710 to A$1,600.

15
00:00:39,616 --> 00:00:42,319
And bad timing could cost her a whole year.

16
00:00:43,219 --> 00:00:45,295
And everyone has an opinion.

17
00:00:45,415 --> 00:00:47,353
The news says: "record high".

18
00:00:47,473 --> 00:00:51,128
Her agent says: "Apply early, the rules keep changing."

19
00:00:51,248 --> 00:00:54,044
Her friends say: "Rent here is out of control."

20
00:00:54,164 --> 00:00:58,384
Her head trusts the official numbers. But her heart trusts her friends.

21
00:00:58,504 --> 00:01:04,361
We also made this story for agents like Daniel, who copy charts straight into client slides.

22
00:01:04,481 --> 00:01:08,133
So every chart here says what it counts, and what it leaves out.

23
00:01:09,033 --> 00:01:12,094
Our evidence comes from five public datasets.

24
00:01:12,214 --> 00:01:18,043
From the Department of Education, Home Affairs, and the Australian Bureau of Statistics.

25
00:01:18,163 --> 00:01:26,386
We give every line the same starting point, 2019 = 100, so big and small numbers can be compared fairly.

26
00:01:26,506 --> 00:01:29,721
Now, let's answer Mei's questions, one by one.

27
00:01:30,921 --> 00:01:34,012
Question 1: Is "record high" even about me?

28
00:01:34,132 --> 00:01:39,740
Here's the trick. The headline counts enrolments. That's everyone who is already studying.

29
00:01:39,860 --> 00:01:42,571
Think of it as everyone inside the airport.

30
00:01:42,691 --> 00:01:46,759
But Mei cares about commencements: the people who are just landing.

31
00:01:46,879 --> 00:01:51,265
Compared with 2019, enrolments are up 8.8%.

32
00:01:51,385 --> 00:01:54,866
But new arrivals are down 17.6%.

33
00:01:54,986 --> 00:01:58,030
The total came back. The new intake did not.

34
00:01:59,830 --> 00:02:02,674
Question 2: Where would I study and live?

35
00:02:02,794 --> 00:02:04,694
Sydney is not one place.

36
00:02:04,814 --> 00:02:10,033
60.1% of known enrolments sit in just one area: City & Inner South.

37
00:02:10,153 --> 00:02:13,532
The top three areas hold 85.8%.

38
00:02:14,132 --> 00:02:16,708
And each area has its own subjects.

39
00:02:16,828 --> 00:02:21,083
In the city, Business leads, with 81,426 students.

40
00:02:21,203 --> 00:02:26,817
But in Outer South West, Health beats Business: 614 to 48.

41
00:02:26,937 --> 00:02:30,806
One note: this shows where students study, not where they live.

42
00:02:32,406 --> 00:02:35,312
Question 3: When is it safe to apply?

43
00:02:35,432 --> 00:02:40,402
The visa grant rate goes up and down. But every big dip sits next to a date.

44
00:02:40,522 --> 00:02:44,159
March 2024: the Genuine Student test.

45
00:02:44,279 --> 00:02:46,893
July 2024: the visa fee more than doubles.

46
00:02:47,013 --> 00:02:49,402
December 2024: new caps on enrolments (MD111).

47
00:02:49,522 --> 00:02:54,121
Lining up in time does not prove cause. But it shows Mei what to watch.

48
00:02:54,921 --> 00:02:56,585
Her sector matters too.

49
00:02:56,705 --> 00:03:02,963
Higher Education carries the record headline. Yet only 22.3% of its students are new.

50
00:03:03,083 --> 00:03:05,941
In English language courses (ELICOS), it's about half: 49.9%.

51
00:03:07,541 --> 00:03:09,899
Question 4: Can I afford it?

52
00:03:10,019 --> 00:03:16,808
From 2019 to 2025, overall prices in Sydney rose 22.7%.

53
00:03:16,928 --> 00:03:18,862
Rents rose 15.1%.

54
00:03:18,982 --> 00:03:22,094
So overall prices grew faster than the number of students.

55
00:03:22,214 --> 00:03:24,840
But this is an index, not a price tag.

56
00:03:24,960 --> 00:03:29,774
It can't give Mei a monthly budget. So the honest move is to plan a buffer.

57
00:03:31,274 --> 00:03:33,448
And this is Mei's "aha" moment.

58
00:03:33,568 --> 00:03:36,663
These four answers can't be judged one by one.

59
00:03:36,783 --> 00:03:40,339
Place, timing, sector and cost have to work together.

60
00:03:40,459 --> 00:03:43,001
The headline was only the starting point.

61
00:03:44,301 --> 00:03:45,922
So here's Mei's plan.

62
00:03:46,042 --> 00:03:49,410
Time the application against the policy timeline.

63
00:03:49,530 --> 00:03:51,706
Pick an area, not a whole city.

64
00:03:51,826 --> 00:03:54,394
Budget a buffer, not one fixed number.

65
00:03:54,514 --> 00:03:57,371
And check how much of her sector is new students.

66
00:03:57,491 --> 00:04:03,379
What she still needs: her own course fees, real rent quotes, and the next policy update.

67
00:04:03,499 --> 00:04:05,892
The data can't decide those for her.

68
00:04:07,392 --> 00:04:11,760
In the end, Mei doesn't decide because of one headline, or one number.

69
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:14,453
Sydney becomes a choice she can plan for.

70
00:04:14,573 --> 00:04:16,311
That's the price of arrival.

